            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"Spiritual rest and salvation in Christ, as well as the ultimate eschatological rest for the people of God in the new creation. It symbolizes the cessation of striving for one's own justification and entering into the finished work of God.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Hebrews 4:1-11\",\"Colossians 2:16-17\",\"Genesis 2:2-3\",\"Matthew 11:28-30\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"A symbol of the new creation and resurrection, fulfilled practically by observing Sunday (the Lord's Day) as the Christian Sabbath.\",\"basis\":[\"Acts 20:7\",\"1 Corinthians 16:2\",\"Revelation 1:10\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"A perpetual moral command and covenant sign of loyalty to the Creator, to be observed literally on the seventh day (Saturday).\",\"basis\":[\"Exodus 20:8-11\",\"Exodus 31:13-17\",\"Ezekiel 20:12\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"},{\"meaning\":\"A symbol of socio-economic liberation, mercy, and egalitarianism, freeing marginalized people, slaves, and animals from ceaseless labor, pointing to the Jubilee.\",\"basis\":[\"Deuteronomy 5:12-15\",\"Leviticus 25:1-12\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"While there is a strong theological consensus that the Sabbath typologically symbolizes spiritual rest in Christ and the final eschatological state, there remains significant debate regarding its ongoing practical application, such as Sabbatarian Sunday observance versus complete fulfillment and abrogation as a ceremonial law.\"}",
